think Chose the crux-first next rung the card itself named first: the whole-tree limb-frame decomposition over every bridge at once. Where cluster_subtree_frames read ONE cut, this rung reads EVERY seam together and joins two prior readings -- the count-balance (cluster_mst_subtrees) and the spatial cleanliness (cluster_subtree_frames.frames_overlap) -- so a surface offers the drag-apart handle at the RIGHT seam, not an arbitrary one.
think The decisive crux is a genuine divergence, not a mechanical aggregation: a COUNT balance and a SPATIAL cleanliness are two different optima, so the seam that splits the counts most evenly and the seam that splits the plane most cleanly can be OPPOSITE edges. On the star-and-tail (reused cluster_subtree_frames' exact geometry so the cross-checks are grounded) the count-most-balanced seam L1-L4 over-claims in space while the only clean seam L4-L5 is the count-worst; on a collinear path every seam is clean and the two optima coincide. Hand-computed both trees and every cut (python3 absent on the pier). GREEN on the FIRST full metal run, no reds.
